== By contents (GBOS) or index (ECO) ==
{{quote|Back in 2005 I was the founder of the Chess Opening Theory wikibook. Thirteen years later, I've gotten around to writing a contents page for it. There is no deadline!|sign=[[User:Chi Sigma]]|source=[https://en.wikibooks.org/w/index.php?title=User:Chi_Sigma&oldid=3364207 (src)]}}

The [[Chess Opening Theory/Great Big Opening Survey|Great Big Opening Survey]] page lists the most frequently occurring opening lines. Given that [[w:Shannon number|the number of possible chess games is mind-bogglingly huge]], this list gives the most common moves that this Wikibook ''ought'' to cover. 

The survey lists the popular moves based on opening databases. The cut-off for inclusion is that a move must appear in at least 0.4% (for the shortlist, 0.2% for the longlist) as many games in a database as the database lists for 1. e4. 1. e4 accounts for about 50% of all first moves, so this means that the move must occur in about 0.1% of games in a (sufficiently large) database to be listed.

=== ECO codes ===
When the [[w:Encyclopaedia of Chess Openings|''Encyclopaedia of Chess Openings'']] (ECO) was written in the 1970s, they came up with a new system for organising openings. Each opening falls under an ECO code, which is a letter from A-E (depending on which of the five volumes of the ECO the opening was printed in) then a two digit number from 00-99. 

If there is a particular opening you want to look at and you know its ECO code, you can look it up in the ECO code indexes:

* [[Chess Opening Theory/ECO volume A|Volume A]], flank openings.
* [[Chess Opening Theory/ECO volume B|Volume B]], semi-open games other than the French.
* [[Chess Opening Theory/ECO volume C|Volume C]], open games and the French.
* [[Chess Opening Theory/ECO volume D|Volume D]], closed and semi-closed games.
* [[Chess Opening Theory/ECO volume E|Volume E]], the Indian defences. 

